1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
In the gene for one chain of haemoglobin,

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,120
change one base in the sixth codon, and

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,800
you can get sickle cell disease.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:09,840
Change a different base in that same codon,

5
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:11,760
and the amino acid stays the same.

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,200
Change one base in codon thirty-nine, and the

7
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,320
chain stops partway, causing a blood disorder called

8
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:19,600
beta thalassaemia.

9
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,800
Every one of these is a single base.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,120
So why are the results so different?

11
00:00:26,334 --> 00:00:28,893
Each of these is a point mutation, a

12
00:00:28,974 --> 00:00:31,773
change to one base, or a few bases,

13
00:00:31,814 --> 00:00:33,053
in a gene's DNA.

14
00:00:33,694 --> 00:00:35,373
There are three ways it can happen.

15
00:00:35,934 --> 00:00:38,733
A substitution swaps one base for another.

16
00:00:39,214 --> 00:00:42,173
An insertion adds a base, and a deletion

17
00:00:42,334 --> 00:00:43,213
removes one.

18
00:00:43,854 --> 00:00:46,893
They come from copying mistakes, or from mutagens,

19
00:00:47,014 --> 00:00:49,053
like the base analogues and the chemicals that

20
00:00:49,134 --> 00:00:51,453
slip between bases, from the last video.

21
00:00:52,094 --> 00:00:55,293
Remember, a ribosome reads the mRNA copy of

22
00:00:55,374 --> 00:00:57,933
the gene in codons of three, starting at

23
00:00:58,014 --> 00:00:58,973
the start codon.

24
00:00:59,534 --> 00:01:01,533
So the real question is, what does the

25
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:03,533
change do to the codons?

26
00:01:04,734 --> 00:01:06,333
Start with substitutions.

27
00:01:06,814 --> 00:01:10,093
A substitution inside a coding codon changes that

28
00:01:10,214 --> 00:01:12,973
codon, so read the new codon to see

29
00:01:13,054 --> 00:01:15,773
what it codes for: the same amino acid,

30
00:01:15,801 --> 00:01:17,453
a different one, or a stop.

31
00:01:18,094 --> 00:01:20,813
Most amino acids have more than one codon,

32
00:01:20,974 --> 00:01:23,533
often differing only in the third base.

33
00:01:23,654 --> 00:01:26,733
So G A G becoming G A A

34
00:01:27,054 --> 00:01:28,653
still codes for glutamate.

35
00:01:28,894 --> 00:01:30,813
That's a silent mutation.

36
00:01:30,894 --> 00:01:33,293
The base sequence changed, but the amino acid

37
00:01:33,374 --> 00:01:34,413
sequence didn't.

38
00:01:35,634 --> 00:01:38,513
Now change the middle base instead, so G

39
00:01:38,594 --> 00:01:41,073
A G becomes G U G.

40
00:01:41,554 --> 00:01:44,113
The chart gives valine, not glutamate.

41
00:01:44,514 --> 00:01:48,313
Glutamate carries a charge, but valine avoids water.

42
00:01:48,594 --> 00:01:50,913
One amino acid is swapped for another, and

43
00:01:50,994 --> 00:01:52,673
that's a missense mutation.

44
00:01:53,154 --> 00:01:54,673
You've seen where this one leads.

45
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:57,953
With two copies, the changed haemoglobin sticks together

46
00:01:58,034 --> 00:02:00,513
when oxygen is low, and red cells bend

47
00:02:00,634 --> 00:02:01,553
into sickles.

48
00:02:01,794 --> 00:02:04,553
But not every missense change matters this much.

49
00:02:04,754 --> 00:02:07,233
It depends on where the amino acid sits,

50
00:02:07,274 --> 00:02:09,073
and how different the new one is.

51
00:02:10,301 --> 00:02:13,020
A third kind of substitution creates an early

52
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:13,780
stop.

53
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:17,340
In codon thirty-nine, C A G codes for

54
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:18,140
glutamine.

55
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:20,860
Change the C to U, and it reads

56
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:24,300
U A G, a stop codon, U Are

57
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:25,100
Gone.

58
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:27,820
The ribosome stops there, so the chain is

59
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:30,860
cut short after thirty-eight amino acids, and it

60
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:33,020
can't fold into a working beta chain.

61
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,260
That's a nonsense mutation.

62
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:38,220
A person with two copies makes no normal

63
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:40,860
beta chains, so they have too little working

64
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:41,820
haemoglobin.

65
00:02:43,034 --> 00:02:45,833
Insertions and deletions act very differently.

66
00:02:46,394 --> 00:02:48,473
Here's a short message that codes for five

67
00:02:48,554 --> 00:02:49,753
amino acids.

68
00:02:50,074 --> 00:02:53,033
Now delete one base from the second codon.

69
00:02:53,354 --> 00:02:55,673
The ribosome still reads in threes from the

70
00:02:55,754 --> 00:02:58,633
start codon, so every codon from there on

71
00:02:58,754 --> 00:03:01,193
is different, and the amino acids after it

72
00:03:01,274 --> 00:03:02,233
are mostly wrong.

73
00:03:02,794 --> 00:03:04,953
A stop codon often turns up early, too.

74
00:03:05,514 --> 00:03:08,433
That's a frameshift, because the reading frame, the

75
00:03:08,474 --> 00:03:10,633
way the bases are grouped into codons, has

76
00:03:10,714 --> 00:03:11,193
shifted.

77
00:03:11,834 --> 00:03:14,233
Inserting one base does the same thing.

78
00:03:15,434 --> 00:03:16,913
Here's a way to picture it.

79
00:03:17,194 --> 00:03:19,513
Read the sentence THE FAT CAT ATE THE

80
00:03:19,594 --> 00:03:21,433
RAT in three-letter words.

81
00:03:21,994 --> 00:03:24,873
Delete the F, keep reading in threes, and

82
00:03:24,954 --> 00:03:27,513
every word after it turns to gibberish.

83
00:03:27,834 --> 00:03:30,393
A cell does the same, except a shifted

84
00:03:30,474 --> 00:03:33,113
frame still makes amino acids, just the wrong

85
00:03:33,194 --> 00:03:34,233
ones.

86
00:03:34,314 --> 00:03:36,873
Now delete three bases in a row instead,

87
00:03:36,954 --> 00:03:38,873
a whole codon's worth.

88
00:03:38,954 --> 00:03:41,113
The frame lines up again, so the protein

89
00:03:41,354 --> 00:03:43,913
loses one amino acid, and everything after it

90
00:03:43,994 --> 00:03:45,273
reads normally.

91
00:03:45,674 --> 00:03:48,393
Inserting three bases works the same way, adding

92
00:03:48,474 --> 00:03:49,353
one amino acid.

93
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:52,886
So what decides how much a point mutation

94
00:03:52,967 --> 00:03:53,526
matters?

95
00:03:54,087 --> 00:03:55,606
First, its type.

96
00:03:56,087 --> 00:03:59,126
A substitution inside a coding codon changes that

97
00:03:59,207 --> 00:04:02,566
codon, so it's silent, swaps one amino acid,

98
00:04:02,687 --> 00:04:04,086
or adds an early stop.

99
00:04:04,727 --> 00:04:08,326
A one-base insertion or deletion changes every codon

100
00:04:08,407 --> 00:04:08,966
after it.

101
00:04:09,687 --> 00:04:11,286
Second, its position.

102
00:04:11,767 --> 00:04:13,606
A frameshift near the start of a gene

103
00:04:13,687 --> 00:04:16,486
changes almost the whole protein, but one near

104
00:04:16,527 --> 00:04:18,966
the end changes only the last few amino

105
00:04:19,047 --> 00:04:20,006
acids.

106
00:04:20,087 --> 00:04:22,966
So "point" describes the size of the change,

107
00:04:23,047 --> 00:04:24,486
not the size of its effect.

108
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:26,740
Two aids.

109
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:31,060
For substitutions, silent means the same amino acid,

110
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:33,860
missense misses the right amino acid and puts

111
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:37,380
in a different one, and nonsense means stop,

112
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,060
so the chain is cut short.

113
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:42,180
And for insertions and deletions, remember the fat

114
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:42,660
cat.

115
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:46,020
Lose one letter, and everything after it shifts.

116
00:04:47,234 --> 00:04:48,673
Two exam questions now.

117
00:04:49,154 --> 00:04:51,873
First, here's part of the mRNA for an

118
00:04:51,954 --> 00:04:55,233
enzyme, three mutated versions of it, and part

119
00:04:55,314 --> 00:04:56,913
of the codon chart.

120
00:04:56,994 --> 00:04:59,393
For each mutant, name the type of point

121
00:04:59,474 --> 00:05:02,833
mutation, and describe its effect on the polypeptide.

122
00:05:03,154 --> 00:05:04,513
Pause here to answer.

123
00:05:14,934 --> 00:05:17,813
Mutant A changed G C A to G

124
00:05:17,894 --> 00:05:20,933
C G, but both code for alanine, so

125
00:05:20,974 --> 00:05:23,573
it's a silent substitution, and the amino acid

126
00:05:23,654 --> 00:05:25,413
sequence is unchanged.

127
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:28,693
In mutant B, U A C became U

128
00:05:28,854 --> 00:05:30,773
A A, a stop codon.

129
00:05:31,174 --> 00:05:34,053
That's a nonsense substitution, so the chain stops

130
00:05:34,174 --> 00:05:35,813
after two amino acids.

131
00:05:36,134 --> 00:05:38,453
Mutant C has lost a base, so it's

132
00:05:38,481 --> 00:05:41,413
a deletion and a frameshift, and every codon

133
00:05:41,494 --> 00:05:42,533
after it changes.

134
00:05:43,014 --> 00:05:46,293
Calling C a substitution is the classic mistake.

135
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:47,980
Second.

136
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:51,420
The most common cystic fibrosis allele has three

137
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,860
bases deleted from the CFTR gene.

138
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:58,380
A common Tay-Sachs allele has four bases inserted

139
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:00,940
into the HEXA gene.

140
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:04,540
Explain why the CFTR protein is missing only

141
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:08,300
one amino acid, while the HEXA protein is

142
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:11,740
changed from the insertion onwards and cut short.

143
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:13,180
Pause here to answer.

144
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,360
Codons are read in threes.

145
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:30,240
Deleting three bases removes exactly one codon's worth,

146
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:32,960
so the reading frame after it is unchanged,

147
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:35,600
and the protein loses just one amino acid.

148
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,800
Four isn't a multiple of three, so the

149
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:40,560
insertion shifts the frame.

150
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,760
Every codon after it is regrouped, and a

151
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:45,440
stop codon soon appears.

152
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:48,400
If you said each change only affects the

153
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:50,960
amino acids where it happens, you've missed the

154
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:51,760
frame.

155
00:06:52,967 --> 00:06:54,886
So when a question gives you a point

156
00:06:54,967 --> 00:06:58,566
mutation, first ask whether it's a substitution, or

157
00:06:58,727 --> 00:07:00,486
an insertion or deletion.

158
00:07:00,967 --> 00:07:03,606
For a substitution, read the new codon on

159
00:07:03,687 --> 00:07:06,486
the chart: the same amino acid, a different

160
00:07:06,647 --> 00:07:07,846
one, or stop.

161
00:07:08,327 --> 00:07:11,206
For an insertion or deletion, count the bases.

162
00:07:11,607 --> 00:07:13,526
If it isn't a multiple of three, the

163
00:07:13,687 --> 00:07:14,566
frame shifts.

164
00:07:15,047 --> 00:07:16,886
Then say where it is, and what that

165
00:07:16,967 --> 00:07:18,206
does to the protein.

166
00:07:18,487 --> 00:07:21,526
Next, what happens when whole chunks of chromosomes

167
00:07:21,607 --> 00:07:22,246
change.

168
00:07:23,467 --> 00:07:24,906
Here are the key notes.

169
00:07:25,147 --> 00:07:27,066
Pause the video if you need more time

170
00:07:27,147 --> 00:07:27,946
to copy them.
